\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces User interface and workflow of the LigPlot3D web application. The clean and minimal interface of LigPlot3D is organized into four functional regions to streamline protein--ligand interaction analysis. (a) Control center: The left-hand panel provides target selection and visualization controls, allowing users to select ligands, adjust representation styles (e.g., cartoon, ball-and-stick), define pocket residue display modes, customize ligand coloring, and filter interaction types such as hydrogen bonds, salt bridges, $\pi $-stacking, hydrophobic contacts, and halogen bonds. (b) Structure input module: Users can upload a local PDB file or directly enter a PDB identifier to rapidly load and visualize a protein structure, enabling seamless integration with publicly available structural data. (c) Main visualization canvas: The central 3D rendering window displays the protein--ligand complex with interactive controls for zooming, rotation, and inspection of atomic interactions. Intermolecular interactions are represented with color-coded dashed lines for intuitive interpretation. (d) Analysis pane: A structured interaction table summarizes detected contacts, including interaction type, distance metrics, ligand atom identifiers, and corresponding protein residues and atoms, facilitating quantitative evaluation and reproducible reporting of binding interactions.}}{10}{figure.caption.17}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:initial}{{1}{10}{User interface and workflow of the LigPlot3D web application. The clean and minimal interface of LigPlot3D is organized into four functional regions to streamline protein--ligand interaction analysis. (a) Control center: The left-hand panel provides target selection and visualization controls, allowing users to select ligands, adjust representation styles (e.g., cartoon, ball-and-stick), define pocket residue display modes, customize ligand coloring, and filter interaction types such as hydrogen bonds, salt bridges, $\pi $-stacking, hydrophobic contacts, and halogen bonds. (b) Structure input module: Users can upload a local PDB file or directly enter a PDB identifier to rapidly load and visualize a protein structure, enabling seamless integration with publicly available structural data. (c) Main visualization canvas: The central 3D rendering window displays the protein--ligand complex with interactive controls for zooming, rotation, and inspection of atomic interactions. Intermolecular interactions are represented with color-coded dashed lines for intuitive interpretation. (d) Analysis pane: A structured interaction table summarizes detected contacts, including interaction type, distance metrics, ligand atom identifiers, and corresponding protein residues and atoms, facilitating quantitative evaluation and reproducible reporting of binding interactions}{figure.caption.17}{}}
